{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975784,"nonce":"BF1+eb/LHp6LfJ4biVCMxt7gXbQ=","title":"Minted AIR constants are invisible to every consumer of the serialized form","message":"`Instr::Int` names a POOL INDEX. A pass needing a constant the module's pool does\nnot hold mints one into `Function::pending_ints` at an index past the pool;\n`Serialized::new_ints` reports them so a caller can extend the pool. Nothing\noutside the air crate's own tests reads `new_ints`.\n\nSo a widened loop -- which mints a vector trip count, a reduction identity and a\nlane offset per lane -- produces `Opcode::Int` indices that these read out of\nbounds: the opcode interpreter (`bytecode.ints[ptr.0]`) and Cranelift's opcode\nlowering path via `cranelift::air::body_for`.\n\nAIR-consuming paths are fine (`int_at`, LLVM materializes `pending_ints` as\nglobals). It is latent, not live -- which functions each path compiles is luck,\nnot an enforced property.\n\nFix: one growable pool the whole process reads, with globally assigned indices,\nso `int_pool_base` stops being a per-function origin two functions can claim.","files":null}]}